#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233952 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{-1/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/4+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{4}+1)}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\,{x}^{3/4}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(\\sqrt[4]{{4}^{3}}-\\sqrt[4]{{1}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(\\sqrt[4]{64}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234034 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}(x+{30}^{∘})-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{sin}x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}(x+{30}^{∘})\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $4$ and then subtracting $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\operatorname{sin}(x+{30}^{∘})\\le 1 \\\\  & -4\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}(x+{30}^{∘})\\le 4 \\\\  & -6\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}(x+{30}^{∘})-2\\le 2 \\\\  & -6\\le f(x)\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)\\in [-6,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234142 :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=9.$ The normal line at the point $(3,\\,3)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(3,\\,3).$  Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{'},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 6x-4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-6x}{-4y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3x}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(3,\\,3)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(3,\\,3)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3x}{2y}\\right)}_{(3,\\,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is $$\n\\displaystyle {m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3/2}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(3,\\,3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{'}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-3) & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0-3)+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(-3)+3 \\\\ y & =2+3 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234143 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $2x{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}({y}^{2})=3{x}^{3}+10$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $2x{y}^{2}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}({y}^{2})) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{3}+10) \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}({y}^{2})) & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10) \\\\ 2(2xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2})+(-2y\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =9{x}^{2} \\\\ 4xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}^{2}+(-2y\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =9{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}(4xy-2y\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =9{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{9{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}}{4xy-2y\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234186 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 1\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If we write the integrand as a power of $x$, we have ${x}^{0}=1.$ Therefore, we can use the power rule with $n=0$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= 1+0 \\\\ &= 1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234248 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right),\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right),\\,-3\\le t\\le 3,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0$ | ${\\color{blue}b}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | ${\\color{blue}c}$ |\n| $y$ | ${\\color{blue}a}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $-a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-3$ into $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\na=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (-3)}{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (-1)}{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (3)}{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n-a+b+c=-(-1)+(-1)+(-1)=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234256 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=-3t,\\,\\,\\,y=2t,\\,-5\\le t\\le 0,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-5$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $15$ | $12$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ | $c$ |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $a$ | $-6$ | $b$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a-b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-4$ into $y=2t.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=2(-4)=-8.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=-2$ into $y=2t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=2(-2)-4.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=0$ into $x=-3t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=-3(0)=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na-b+c=-8-(-4)+0=-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234271 :question/problem "Let $n=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $n$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{n}{|\\,n\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|n| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{41}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $n$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{n}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{41}}\\cdot n \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{41}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{41}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{41}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234272 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$, $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $c=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $3a-(b+4c).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-(b+4c) & =3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234273 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $c=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $a+(2b-2c).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+(2b-2c) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+(2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234296 :question/problem "Differentiate ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{sin}x)+({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{cos}x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234298 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{5/2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2+1}}{(\\frac{5}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}{x}^{7/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{5/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234299 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/7}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/7+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/7}}{\\left(\\frac{6}{7}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234300 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{10}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=10$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{10}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{10+1}}{10+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{11}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{11})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{11}\\cdot 11{x}^{10} \\\\ &= {x}^{10}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234325 :question/problem "![](images/4c/4ce20d9d732816efda0a2fd3aebabc467f9f2c73d0b6037f4ad8f95d8e8ef1e7.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/6e/6e070d26915bcb11118f5a23f36b80038d5dd865e32a8517a12c10939ca02f0b.png)\n\nFirst, we find the area of $△ABD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{85}^{∘} \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{85}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈23.908\\,7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area of $△BCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BCD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 14\\cdot 16\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{36.4}^{∘} \\\\  & =112\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{36.4}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈66.462\\,9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BCD} \\\\  & ≈23.908\\,7+66.462\\,9 \\\\  & ≈90.371\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $90.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234355 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-2t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=\\sqrt{t}-4,\\,t\\ge 0,\n$$\n\ndetermine the range of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}-2t-1 \\\\  & ={(t-1)}^{2}-{1}^{2}-1 \\\\  & ={(t-1)}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${(t-1)}^{2}\\ge 0,$ we can conclude that $x\\ge -2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235382 :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)=2{t}^{2}+3,\\,h(g(t))=g(t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =(2{t}^{2}+3)^{′}=4t, \\\\ {h}^{′}(g(t)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}] & ={h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={h}^{′}(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235431 :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal{C}$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\nSlope $\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}+h\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2{e}^{\\pi }.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235444 :question/problem "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{x}$ equals\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$+\\,\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[5]{x}={x}^{1/5}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/5+1}}{(\\frac{1}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/5}}{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5}{x}^{6/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/5}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235461 :question/problem "Find the norm of the fundamental vector product for the surface $\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta ),$ found in the previous step.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{\\theta }^{'}‖=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for a surface with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}.$\n\nIn part 4, we got the surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ⟩,\\hspace{1em}x\\in [0,1],\\hspace{1em}\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}4x\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}4x\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{\\theta }^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂\\theta }(x),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂\\theta }(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂\\theta }(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{\\theta }^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 4x\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & 4x\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}4x\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & 4x\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ -2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4x\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 0 & 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4x\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{x}^{3}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +8{x}^{3}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{x}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ),\\hspace{0.222em}-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{x}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding norm is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{\\theta }^{'}‖ & =\\sqrt{(8{x}^{3})^{2}+(-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{x}^{6}+4{x}^{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +4{x}^{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{x}^{6}+4{x}^{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{x}^{6}+4{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235462 :question/problem "If we denote our surface of revolution by $S,$ then the total electric charge of the shell is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle Q={∬}_{S}^{}d(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nPreviously, we determined that our surface can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ⟩,\\hspace{1em}x\\in [0,1],\\hspace{1em}\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nand the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{\\theta }^{'}‖=2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta )$ into $d(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta )) & =\\frac{x({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x((2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}d(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}d(\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta ))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{\\theta }^{'}‖\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}\\cdot 2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}8{x}^{7}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8{x}^{7}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[{x}^{8}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235479 :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal{C}$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\nSlope $\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}+h\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2{e}^{\\pi }.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235490 :question/problem "What is the slope $m$ of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB}?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$m\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "As we found previously, the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,-2)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(1,4),$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-(-2)}{1-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{-2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235496 :question/problem "Write the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a circle with radius $r$  centered at the point $(a,b)$ in rectangular coordinates in standard form is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the circle is centered at $(3,0)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=3$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the circle in rectangular coordinates is \n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235501 :question/problem "Construct and simplify an integral representing the arc length of this circle using polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235513 :question/problem "Find the dot product of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}$ and $\\mathbf{r}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{r}=\\hspace{0.167em}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The dot product of two vectors given in component form can be computed by summing up the products of corresponding components of vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{r} & ={q}_{x}\\cdot {r}_{x}+{q}_{y}\\cdot {r}_{y}+{q}_{z}\\cdot {r}_{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom part 1, we have $\\mathbf{q}=⟨0,-3,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨0,4,3⟩.$ Therefore, the formula for the dot product gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{r} & =0\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0-12+6 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235522 :question/problem "The divergence $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}$ of the velocity field represents the scalar rate of expansion of the fluid per unit of area at a given point. Find the corresponding expansion rate at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe required rate of expansion is $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{V}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{V}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{V}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{V}=\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy) \\\\  & =2xy+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}$ at the point $(1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)} & =2(1)(1)+2(1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235523 :question/problem "Find the partial derivatives of $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+2x) \\\\  & =2y+2 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+2x) \\\\  & =2x+0 \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)} & =2(1)+2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)} & =2(1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235524 :question/problem "Find the direction of the greatest rate of expansion of the fluid at the point $(1,1).$ Do not normalize the answer.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe required direction is $\\mathrm{∇}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)}=⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "In part 2, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient at $(1,1)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨4,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235525 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe required directional derivative is ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(1,1)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)=\\mathrm{∇}f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{5}(-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(1,1)=\\mathrm{∇}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)}=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(1,1) & =\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(1,1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =(4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}(-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 4}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235527 :question/problem "The scalar curl of a plane velocity field is called the **vorticity,** denoted $\\omega .$ The vorticity describes a measure of the local rotation of the field at a given point. Find the vorticity of $\\mathbf{V}$ at $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\omega (1,1)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{V}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{V}$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{V}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $P={x}^{2}y$ and $Q=2xy.$ Therefore, the scalar curl (i.e., the vorticity) of $\\mathbf{V}$ at $(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\omega  & =\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2y-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\omega (1,1) & =(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =2(1)-(1)^{2} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235528 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n>$\\mathbf{F}$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor our vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ the domain is $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=a{y}^{3}-1,\\,Q=3x{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3a{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a{y}^{2} & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3a & =3 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235529 :question/problem "$\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn part 1, we found that $a=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=({y}^{3}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x{y}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0+6xy \\\\  & =6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235530 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that $a=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=({y}^{3}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{3}-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({y}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{y}^{3}-x+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & =3x{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =1 \\\\ g(y) & =y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-x+y+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235531 :question/problem "![](images/64/642a546fc498dfb0f01c0729959f9f68b8dc2d142d966673f204eda910f19211.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\nConsider the square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ shown above. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}^{}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{i}.$\n\n![](images/19/1939a274191b89f69e51a929654489ed9038df0313f6e47b15e0ff55ad813066.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(({y}^{3}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{y}^{3}-1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235533 :question/problem "![](images/6f/6fd34e9070cd9555ba790e7300bb78b4d19f1b71b231e8a92f4e8f306c3d4623.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\nThe region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. \nIn polar coordinates, the following integral can be written as\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the disk enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235535 :question/problem "$\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+2x+0 \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235537 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe required rate is $$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nIn part 1, we found that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} &=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}2x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235539 :question/problem "$\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n$⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {z}^{2} & 2xy & x{y}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xy)]\\mathbf{i}-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2})]\\mathbf{j}+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})]\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2xy-0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-({y}^{2}-2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2y-0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-({y}^{2}-2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2xy,\\hspace{0.222em}2z-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235540 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the top (tilted) surface of our solid. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, as shown in the diagram. Use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation of the air along $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe required circulation is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot dr\\,={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our top (tilted) surface ${S}_{T}$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4-x-y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-x-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨2xy,\\hspace{0.222em}2z-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2y⟩{|}_{z=4-x-y} \\\\  & =⟨2xy,\\hspace{0.222em}2(4-x-y)-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{2xy}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{8-2x-2y-{y}^{2}}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{2y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{T}}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2xy)\\cdot (-1)-(8-2x-2y-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-1)+2y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0f9cb64d0ae1c3615df5a05c9f580bb9c74f6529efd732cfd83e1f4328ff8a3.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}]}
